(224360, 12608, 3599, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club.<br/>I look at this photo and see a lot of potential.  It to me is already a very good photo.  But what if you had been able to get in closer and framed only one of the smaller branches and needles.  I think the photo may have been more dramatic and wouldn''t lose a connection to rhythm.  Do you have pine cones to go along with this tree or is it some other tree?  Maybe a macro of the surface of the pine cone.  There''s a lot of rhythm there.  I''m not saying you didn''t meet the challenge I''m just giving you alternatives on what i think may have been available for you to photograph.  I like the framing and the fact you went with a silhouette.  That is a nice blue with the clouds giving it a pleasent touch.  Again, you have a good photo here that may or not have been better if you had gotten in a little closer.  I hope this helps some.  I''ve been having great difficulty writting these lately.  Feel free to e-mail me if you have questions.  Good luck in future challenges.</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club.I look at this photo and see a lot of potential.  It to me is already a very good photo.  But what if you had been able to get in closer and framed only one of the smaller branches and needles.  I think the photo may have been more dramatic and wouldn''t lose a connection to rhythm.  Do you have pine cones to go along with this tree or is it some other tree?  Maybe a macro of the surface of the pine cone.  There''s a lot of rhythm there.  I''m not saying you didn''t meet the challenge I''m just giving you alternatives on what i think may have been available for you to photograph.  I like the framing and the fact you went with a silhouette.  That is a nice blue with the clouds giving it a pleasent touch.  Again, you have a good photo here that may or not have been better if you had gotten in a little closer.  I hope this helps some.  I''ve been having great difficulty writting these lately.  Feel free to e-mail me if you have questions.  Good luck in future challenges.', '2003-02-25 17:38:07.000000', NULL, 0),
(224391, 12646, 4482, '<td valign="top">Congrats Bod! Great job! You have a a good photographic eye!</td>', 'Congrats Bod! Great job! You have a a good photographic eye!', '2003-02-25 19:05:32.000000', NULL, 0),
(224447, 12666, 3607, '<td valign="top">thanks for all the comments.  unfortunately this is a photo i know could have been better, but it was TOO COLD to hang around and take tons of photos.  sorry.  i took a couple straight on and could only capture two columns of escapes, which did not quite, to me, represent the contest as much as this photo did, with row after row of fire escapes.  here is an <a href="//www.pbase.com/image/7393753" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">older photo</a> that i took a while ago that inspired me to wander back down there and find this image again . . . but as i said, it was just toooo coooold to find the right image.  given the many weaknesses of the photo, i''m pretty happy with the outcome (although i think the angle that the buildings recede from the starting point is too harsh to cut out the bottom any more than i already did).</td>', 'thanks for all the comments.  unfortunately this is a photo i know could have been better, but it was TOO COLD to hang around and take tons of photos.  sorry.  i took a couple straight on and could only capture two columns of escapes, which did not quite, to me, represent the contest as much as this photo did, with row after row of fire escapes.  here is an older photo that i took a while ago that inspired me to wander back down there and find this image again . . . but as i said, it was just toooo coooold to find the right image.  given the many weaknesses of the photo, i''m pretty happy with the outcome (although i think the angle that the buildings recede from the starting point is too harsh to cut out the bottom any more than i already did).', '2003-02-25 20:57:39.000000', NULL, 0),
(224472, 12636, 2716, '<td valign="top">This was my favourite this week, by far.  Should have taken 1st place.  Awesome shot.</td>', 'This was my favourite this week, by far.  Should have taken 1st place.  Awesome shot.', '2003-02-25 21:41:00.000000', NULL, 0),
(224482, 12602, 2170, '<td valign="top">CRITIQUE CLUB!<br/> <br/>There''s really not much here for me to say.  This is a well-done image, perfect in black and white, btw, and really makes me miss my crew days at IU.<br/> <br/>My main irk is in the framing -- I''m not sure why the person on the left got cropped out (or just left out of the shot), but it does seem somewhat abrupt and leaves his oars out hanging.  I would also like to see this with a little bit more of a severe contrast to see if the waterbreaks could be more like whitecaps and generally make the boat separated from the water more.  Did that make sense?<br/> <br/>Lovely photo.  I can''t wait to shoot outside again.<br/> <br/>Rob</td>', 'CRITIQUE CLUB! There''s really not much here for me to say.  This is a well-done image, perfect in black and white, btw, and really makes me miss my crew days at IU. My main irk is in the framing -- I''m not sure why the person on the left got cropped out (or just left out of the shot), but it does seem somewhat abrupt and leaves his oars out hanging.  I would also like to see this with a little bit more of a severe contrast to see if the waterbreaks could be more like whitecaps and generally make the boat separated from the water more.  Did that make sense? Lovely photo.  I can''t wait to shoot outside again. Rob', '2003-02-25 21:59:17.000000', NULL, 0),
(224484, 12629, 2170, '<td valign="top">CRITIQUE CLUB!<br/> <br/>See, now there you have it.  This was one of my top picks for the challenge.  I always end up critiquing the photos that I think were really good to begin with.<br/> <br/>Two comments here, really.  First -- the shadows on the lower right of the wires.  I''m not sure what the background is here, but the shadows blend right into it.  I would like to see them pop from the background more -- if for nothing else than to just give a sense of depth.  Secondly, and you may think I''m nuts, but for this challenge I think a closer crop on the top and the bottom (so that just the bench back is showing) would be better.  This would fill the frame with the "rhythm" that is, after all, the subject.<br/> <br/>Nicely done.  If you''re aching for more snow to shoot, I''ve got 14 inches on top of 1.5" of ice in my yard.  Knock yourself out.  BYO shovel.  :)<br/> <br/>Rob</td>', 'CRITIQUE CLUB! See, now there you have it.  This was one of my top picks for the challenge.  I always end up critiquing the photos that I think were really good to begin with. Two comments here, really.  First -- the shadows on the lower right of the wires.  I''m not sure what the background is here, but the shadows blend right into it.  I would like to see them pop from the background more -- if for nothing else than to just give a sense of depth.  Secondly, and you may think I''m nuts, but for this challenge I think a closer crop on the top and the bottom (so that just the bench back is showing) would be better.  This would fill the frame with the "rhythm" that is, after all, the subject. Nicely done.  If you''re aching for more snow to shoot, I''ve got 14 inches on top of 1.5" of ice in my yard.  Knock yourself out.  BYO shovel.  :) Rob', '2003-02-25 22:03:15.000000', NULL, 0),
(224493, 12740, 2170, '<td valign="top">CRITIQUE CLUB!<br/> <br/>I hate critiquing photos of people I know -- I''m just sure they''re going to get upset.  Sorry in advance.<br/> <br/>What I like:<br/>I love the ducks in motion -- their trails are really just perfect for this challenge, although it''s something I probably wouldn''t have thought of.  Very enjoyable idea.<br/> <br/>What I don''t like:<br/>The breaks in the water -- I can''t tell what they are.  Shallow water around grasses?  Sandbars?  I don''t know.  Unfortunately, they detract from the ducks which are the focus.  Also, the two ducks going in similar but definitely different directions throws off the "rhythm" of the photo.  If I didn''t know what the challenge topic was, I''d probably not pick up on rhythm being the theme.<br/> <br/>Suggestions:<br/>Maybe zooming in closer on one duck and focusing the frame on his wake?  Or, if multiple ducks were the goal, maybe capturing them without the grasses would help the negative space define the wakes so they''d stand out more?<br/> <br/>Very nice idea, though.  I do think that the wakes were a great interpretation of the topic.<br/> <br/>:)<br/>Rob</td>', 'CRITIQUE CLUB! I hate critiquing photos of people I know -- I''m just sure they''re going to get upset.  Sorry in advance. What I like:I love the ducks in motion -- their trails are really just perfect for this challenge, although it''s something I probably wouldn''t have thought of.  Very enjoyable idea. What I don''t like:The breaks in the water -- I can''t tell what they are.  Shallow water around grasses?  Sandbars?  I don''t know.  Unfortunately, they detract from the ducks which are the focus.  Also, the two ducks going in similar but definitely different directions throws off the "rhythm" of the photo.  If I didn''t know what the challenge topic was, I''d probably not pick up on rhythm being the theme. Suggestions:Maybe zooming in closer on one duck and focusing the frame on his wake?  Or, if multiple ducks were the goal, maybe capturing them without the grasses would help the negative space define the wakes so they''d stand out more? Very nice idea, though.  I do think that the wakes were a great interpretation of the topic. :)Rob', '2003-02-25 22:09:54.000000', NULL, 0),
