(224717, 12699, 472, '<td valign="top">congratulations ! :)</td>', 'congratulations ! :)', '2003-02-26 09:17:28.000000', NULL, 0),
(224746, 12726, 1280, '<td valign="top">Critique Club Comments<br/>This is an interesting picture for the texture and shape.  I really like abstracts.  The cropping seems to work well, following the size of the lemon slice. <br/>The curvy lines of the fruit are appealing, and my eyes follow along the lines nicely, in a wavy pattern.<br/>On a personal level, I would have preferred the natural coloring of the fruit...yellow or orange?  But I do understand it is completely up to the artist, in this case you.  <br/>Overall I like this.<br/>Regards,<br/>Grayce</td>', 'Critique Club CommentsThis is an interesting picture for the texture and shape.  I really like abstracts.  The cropping seems to work well, following the size of the lemon slice. The curvy lines of the fruit are appealing, and my eyes follow along the lines nicely, in a wavy pattern.On a personal level, I would have preferred the natural coloring of the fruit...yellow or orange?  But I do understand it is completely up to the artist, in this case you.  Overall I like this.Regards,Grayce', '2003-02-26 10:35:34.000000', NULL, 0),
(224751, 12752, 6855, '<td valign="top">CRITIQUE CLUB<br/> <br/>WOW!  Okay, I have been staring at this for quite some time, and frankly, I LOVE this photo.  It''s absolutely gorgeous.<br/> <br/>LIGHTING: Beautiful.  The only nit-picky critique I have is that the focus falls on the windows at the end which seem like they should have more to them, because they are so bright. Obviously, this couldn''t be corrected with the sunlight, but I would like to see more lighting on the books.<br/> <br/>CHALLENGE: Definitely met.  Wonderful rhythmic look.  Maybe a bit "choppy" - I actually think this would have been perfect for "leading lines"<br/> <br/>TECHNICAL:  I see no problems at all with your settings<br/> <br/>OVERALL:  I absolutely love this, so please don''t think of this as a short critique. It''s hard to critique something that is near perfect. Adjust the lighting on the windows (now that you can edit) and you have yourself a treasure!</td>', 'CRITIQUE CLUB WOW!  Okay, I have been staring at this for quite some time, and frankly, I LOVE this photo.  It''s absolutely gorgeous. LIGHTING: Beautiful.  The only nit-picky critique I have is that the focus falls on the windows at the end which seem like they should have more to them, because they are so bright. Obviously, this couldn''t be corrected with the sunlight, but I would like to see more lighting on the books. CHALLENGE: Definitely met.  Wonderful rhythmic look.  Maybe a bit "choppy" - I actually think this would have been perfect for "leading lines" TECHNICAL:  I see no problems at all with your settings OVERALL:  I absolutely love this, so please don''t think of this as a short critique. It''s hard to critique something that is near perfect. Adjust the lighting on the windows (now that you can edit) and you have yourself a treasure!', '2003-02-26 10:51:09.000000', NULL, 0),
(224769, 12742, 6855, '<td valign="top">CRITIQUE CLUB<br/> <br/>This photo is absolutely breathtaking, making it very hard to critique! <br/> <br/>CHALLENGE:  For rhythm, this photo may be a bit busy.  There are so many examples of rhythm here that you might want to focus on just one thing, like the pattern in that amazing ceiling.<br/> <br/>TECHNICAL:  I think your camera was set perfectly.  There is a light at the bottom of the photo, and I can''t decide if that''s your flash reflection or someone elses?  This is probably something you''ll want to edit later.<br/> <br/>POST CHALLENGE SUGGESTIONS:  I would stongly considered cropping out the bottom of this piece.  The eyes are automatically drawn to that amazing ceiling and everything else adds an element of clutter.  Don''t get me wrong, this is an absolutely GORGEOUS print that deserved a high score!</td>', 'CRITIQUE CLUB This photo is absolutely breathtaking, making it very hard to critique!  CHALLENGE:  For rhythm, this photo may be a bit busy.  There are so many examples of rhythm here that you might want to focus on just one thing, like the pattern in that amazing ceiling. TECHNICAL:  I think your camera was set perfectly.  There is a light at the bottom of the photo, and I can''t decide if that''s your flash reflection or someone elses?  This is probably something you''ll want to edit later. POST CHALLENGE SUGGESTIONS:  I would stongly considered cropping out the bottom of this piece.  The eyes are automatically drawn to that amazing ceiling and everything else adds an element of clutter.  Don''t get me wrong, this is an absolutely GORGEOUS print that deserved a high score!', '2003-02-26 11:21:16.000000', NULL, 0),
(224925, 12437, 4482, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the CC gang!<br/> <br/>THEME: Yes the photo does have some rhthym but IMO adding a few more would''ve made a stronger impact. Maybe you didn''t have any more. :-) <br/> <br/>COMPOSITION: Having them with the same distance between them would create better rhythm as well. One is overlapping and is distracting to the eye. Remember that rhythm is all about symetry. Cropping is fine to me. <br/> <br/>LIGHTING: It seems a bit underexposed. Maybe decreasing shutter speed or adding more light sources would''ve helped. <br/> <br/>General feeling... unfortunately this shot doesn''t have any WOW factor to me. It would not be a shot that I would print out and hang on my wall. <br/> <br/>I hope this helps!<br/> <br/>Yanik<br/> <br/></td>', 'Greetings from the CC gang! THEME: Yes the photo does have some rhthym but IMO adding a few more would''ve made a stronger impact. Maybe you didn''t have any more. :-)  COMPOSITION: Having them with the same distance between them would create better rhythm as well. One is overlapping and is distracting to the eye. Remember that rhythm is all about symetry. Cropping is fine to me.  LIGHTING: It seems a bit underexposed. Maybe decreasing shutter speed or adding more light sources would''ve helped.  General feeling... unfortunately this shot doesn''t have any WOW factor to me. It would not be a shot that I would print out and hang on my wall.  I hope this helps! Yanik ', '2003-02-26 21:35:22.000000', NULL, 0),
(225002, 12426, 4482, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the CC club!<br/> <br/>First thing first.... GREAT shot! I''m really hard to please when it comes to abstract shots but you succeeded in getting my attention!<br/> <br/>THEME: The choice of the subject and its placement respect the theme.<br/> <br/>COMPOSITION: I get distracted by the white in your frame. I don''t think a frame was necessary for accentuate this shot. I like the chosen angle, it has a tunnel effect which gives it a nice perspective. The choice of colors is great! Black and blue are almost always winners. :-)<br/> <br/>Lighting and focus: Perfect lighting! Great tones. The focus is a bit soft but in this pic it seems to fit.<br/> <br/>GENERAL: not to be too nitpicky but the rhythm impact would''ve been better is the tip of the paper wasn''t cut out of the photo.<br/> <br/>All in all a great shot! Good work!<br/> <br/>Yanik</td>', 'Greetings from the CC club! First thing first.... GREAT shot! I''m really hard to please when it comes to abstract shots but you succeeded in getting my attention! THEME: The choice of the subject and its placement respect the theme. COMPOSITION: I get distracted by the white in your frame. I don''t think a frame was necessary for accentuate this shot. I like the chosen angle, it has a tunnel effect which gives it a nice perspective. The choice of colors is great! Black and blue are almost always winners. :-) Lighting and focus: Perfect lighting! Great tones. The focus is a bit soft but in this pic it seems to fit. GENERAL: not to be too nitpicky but the rhythm impact would''ve been better is the tip of the paper wasn''t cut out of the photo. All in all a great shot! Good work! Yanik', '2003-02-26 22:26:34.000000', NULL, 0),
(225175, 12766, 1143, '<td valign="top">Hmmm. a cabin on the river, huh? Niiiiiiiice.<br/>First off, yes this fits the challenge though I think of it more as a texture shot than a repeating theme. If the leaves were all in a line or going the same direction then it would be more repetitive IMO.<br/>I do like the color, but something about the lighting is bothersome. The leaves are rather shiny to begin with and the little hairs are white so the colors aren''t as prominent as they could be. Focus is good, but because of how close you are to the subject - some areas are soft and the lines aren''t as distinct as they could be. Those little hairs are a bit like animal fur close-up... very hard to focus on and nearly impossible to sharpen in post processing.<br/>I just think this is a difficult subject to shoot b/c of those hairs. You did a decent job though. There just isn''t enough interest to hold someone''s attention for long. The colors are a bit of a blur b/c of the glare off the leaves and there aren''t strong lines or an obvious focal point.<br/>I don''t really like the frame. Unless it can really add to the shot, it''s best to just let the picture stand on its own probably.<br/>Overall, decent shot of what may not have been the best subject. I can see what drew you to it but I''m just not sure it makes a strong photograph. Good luck!</td>', 'Hmmm. a cabin on the river, huh? Niiiiiiiice.First off, yes this fits the challenge though I think of it more as a texture shot than a repeating theme. If the leaves were all in a line or going the same direction then it would be more repetitive IMO.I do like the color, but something about the lighting is bothersome. The leaves are rather shiny to begin with and the little hairs are white so the colors aren''t as prominent as they could be. Focus is good, but because of how close you are to the subject - some areas are soft and the lines aren''t as distinct as they could be. Those little hairs are a bit like animal fur close-up... very hard to focus on and nearly impossible to sharpen in post processing.I just think this is a difficult subject to shoot b/c of those hairs. You did a decent job though. There just isn''t enough interest to hold someone''s attention for long. The colors are a bit of a blur b/c of the glare off the leaves and there aren''t strong lines or an obvious focal point.I don''t really like the frame. Unless it can really add to the shot, it''s best to just let the picture stand on its own probably.Overall, decent shot of what may not have been the best subject. I can see what drew you to it but I''m just not sure it makes a strong photograph. Good luck!', '2003-02-27 08:45:52.000000', NULL, 0),
(225177, 12625, 5728, '<td valign="top">Now that I look at this image again I see what I missed when I first commented on it. Like some others who commented on it I thought it was a girl dancing and the light''s out the back were disco lights. I should really have looked a lot closer though. I see now she is lying on the ground and those are candles following the form of her back. Nice illusion Natasha and very well done!</td>', 'Now that I look at this image again I see what I missed when I first commented on it. Like some others who commented on it I thought it was a girl dancing and the light''s out the back were disco lights. I should really have looked a lot closer though. I see now she is lying on the ground and those are candles following the form of her back. Nice illusion Natasha and very well done!', '2003-02-27 08:52:13.000000', NULL, 0),
(225260, 12662, 2221, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club, Douglas :)<br/> <br/>Composition: The diagonal alignment is a nice composition. I loaded your photo to my image editor and I cropped a bit more from the right just so that the end of the rows of seats is in the upper right corner. Personally I think that would improve the photo and makeit more powerful. I think this also would have allowed you to incease the height of the final image. It looks a bit small like it''s now.<br/> <br/>Lighting: It''s a little bit too light for my taste, but that''s personal preference. Also a nice twist would have been to decrease the saturation of red and only leave the blue tones of the seats saturated. But people think differently on this kind of modifications.<br/> <br/>Focus: Focus is good. Maybe a bot too good, because the photo suffers immensly from the so called moiree effect. I guess you know what I mean. I also struggled with that effect before and I think adding a little blur before downsizing the image helps a bit. Recently there was a discussion about that topic in the DPC forum with more useful tips. Unfortunately I don''t find the link now.<br/> <br/>Challenge: Yes it fits to the challenge theme. The title suggests a double meaning regarding "rhythm". First the repetitive pattern and second that the pattern looks like piano keys. Well, with some imagination you can see a similarity to piano keys but I think it''s not very striking ;)<br/> <br/>Creativity: The photo is not particularly unique or inventive but nevertheless you had a good "photography eye" seeing this perspective.<br/> <br/> <br/>Feel free to email me if you want to discuss the photo more. I also can send you a copy of the image with the modifications I described above (cropping, colours).<br/> <br/>Stephan</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club, Douglas :) Composition: The diagonal alignment is a nice composition. I loaded your photo to my image editor and I cropped a bit more from the right just so that the end of the rows of seats is in the upper right corner. Personally I think that would improve the photo and makeit more powerful. I think this also would have allowed you to incease the height of the final image. It looks a bit small like it''s now. Lighting: It''s a little bit too light for my taste, but that''s personal preference. Also a nice twist would have been to decrease the saturation of red and only leave the blue tones of the seats saturated. But people think differently on this kind of modifications. Focus: Focus is good. Maybe a bot too good, because the photo suffers immensly from the so called moiree effect. I guess you know what I mean. I also struggled with that effect before and I think adding a little blur before downsizing the image helps a bit. Recently there was a discussion about that topic in the DPC forum with more useful tips. Unfortunately I don''t find the link now. Challenge: Yes it fits to the challenge theme. The title suggests a double meaning regarding "rhythm". First the repetitive pattern and second that the pattern looks like piano keys. Well, with some imagination you can see a similarity to piano keys but I think it''s not very striking ;) Creativity: The photo is not particularly unique or inventive but nevertheless you had a good "photography eye" seeing this perspective.  Feel free to email me if you want to discuss the photo more. I also can send you a copy of the image with the modifications I described above (cropping, colours). Stephan', '2003-02-27 10:31:13.000000', NULL, 0),
(225476, 12625, 1024, '<td valign="top">Critique Club Critique <br/> <br/>(1) COMPOSITION (CONTENT) This is a wonderful photograph. The absolute simplicity of the photograph is its real strength. I actually see several repetitions; the lights, the girls back and the panty line.... each one adds to the depth and power of this photograph.  The repetition of form makes this fit both in this challenge, but also in perspective.  <br/> <br/>(2) BACKGROUND The background is very dark here, and in that way does not distract from the subject at all.<br/> <br/>(3) CAMERA WORK ,TECHNICAL The DOF is very good, all of the important parts are in sharp focus. I have read your comments about having a problem with some noise around the candles, and I see a little stray light, but not enough to be distracting.  <br/> <br/>(4) DIGITAL PROCESSING ,TECHNICAL Your post processing is good. Good use of black and white.  Is very reminiscent of an old movie.  You have managed to keep your whites very crisp without losing your blacks.  That is not always easy to do.<br/> <br/>(5)MEETING THE CHALLENGE You have done a marvolous job of meeting the challenge in an origional and refreshing way.<br/> <br/>(6) MY OPINION ON THE PHOTO The subject here is wonderful. The way you executed it is great. This was one of the best of the rhythm pics.  I am happy to see your score reflecting this.  Well done!</td>', 'Critique Club Critique  (1) COMPOSITION (CONTENT) This is a wonderful photograph. The absolute simplicity of the photograph is its real strength. I actually see several repetitions; the lights, the girls back and the panty line.... each one adds to the depth and power of this photograph.  The repetition of form makes this fit both in this challenge, but also in perspective.   (2) BACKGROUND The background is very dark here, and in that way does not distract from the subject at all. (3) CAMERA WORK ,TECHNICAL The DOF is very good, all of the important parts are in sharp focus. I have read your comments about having a problem with some noise around the candles, and I see a little stray light, but not enough to be distracting.   (4) DIGITAL PROCESSING ,TECHNICAL Your post processing is good. Good use of black and white.  Is very reminiscent of an old movie.  You have managed to keep your whites very crisp without losing your blacks.  That is not always easy to do. (5)MEETING THE CHALLENGE You have done a marvolous job of meeting the challenge in an origional and refreshing way. (6) MY OPINION ON THE PHOTO The subject here is wonderful. The way you executed it is great. This was one of the best of the rhythm pics.  I am happy to see your score reflecting this.  Well done!', '2003-02-27 15:14:24.000000', NULL, 0),
(225616, 12394, 6855, '<td valign="top">CRITIQUE CLUB<br/> <br/>This is such an awesome photo!  I don''t know if I''m the right person to critique it because I love it so much!<br/> <br/>LIGHTING:  Very good, but may have provided more interest with some shadows or shading.  That''s just being nit-picky though<br/> <br/>CHALLENGE:  About as rhythmic as you can get!  I find it fascinating how you caught this without flaws disturbing the rhythm of the print.  <br/> <br/>SUBJECT:  I like what you chose because it''s unique.  The earth color is absolutely GORGEOUS!  The red tint makes it so interesting and fun to look at.<br/> <br/>OVERALL:  A really great photo that seems to go on forever.  You should be proud of this one...I honestly can''t think of much I would change.</td>', 'CRITIQUE CLUB This is such an awesome photo!  I don''t know if I''m the right person to critique it because I love it so much! LIGHTING:  Very good, but may have provided more interest with some shadows or shading.  That''s just being nit-picky though CHALLENGE:  About as rhythmic as you can get!  I find it fascinating how you caught this without flaws disturbing the rhythm of the print.   SUBJECT:  I like what you chose because it''s unique.  The earth color is absolutely GORGEOUS!  The red tint makes it so interesting and fun to look at. OVERALL:  A really great photo that seems to go on forever.  You should be proud of this one...I honestly can''t think of much I would change.', '2003-02-27 18:36:19.000000', NULL, 0),
(225765, 12646, 6855, '<td valign="top">CRITIQUE CLUB<br/> <br/>Wow wow and double wow!<br/> <br/>LIGHTING: Brilliant.  Thanks for explaining what you used! I was curious about how you acheived that brilliant blue shine.  I LOVE the lighting, but the inside seems a little dark.  Not enough to make it noticeable, I''m just curious how it would look with the inside lighted.<br/> <br/>CHALLENGE:  This met the challenge perfectly, and your ribbon was very well deserved.<br/> <br/>SUBJECT:  A pen holder?  I had NO idea that''s what this was. I think that''s what makes it so interesting.  I thought it was a very LARGE container, meaning your angle and focus were excellent.  The effect is fantastic.<br/> <br/>OVERALL:  I know I''m supposed to honestly "critique" this, but I just can''t think of anything that needs improving.  Your award was well-deserved.  You not only showed great technique, but an awesome eye as well.  GREAT job!</td>', 'CRITIQUE CLUB Wow wow and double wow! LIGHTING: Brilliant.  Thanks for explaining what you used! I was curious about how you acheived that brilliant blue shine.  I LOVE the lighting, but the inside seems a little dark.  Not enough to make it noticeable, I''m just curious how it would look with the inside lighted. CHALLENGE:  This met the challenge perfectly, and your ribbon was very well deserved. SUBJECT:  A pen holder?  I had NO idea that''s what this was. I think that''s what makes it so interesting.  I thought it was a very LARGE container, meaning your angle and focus were excellent.  The effect is fantastic. OVERALL:  I know I''m supposed to honestly "critique" this, but I just can''t think of anything that needs improving.  Your award was well-deserved.  You not only showed great technique, but an awesome eye as well.  GREAT job!', '2003-02-27 23:45:20.000000', NULL, 0),
(225783, 12420, 6855, '<td valign="top">CRITIQUE CLUB<br/> <br/>LIGHTING:  Great use of the natural lighting - obviously, it would be great if the sky were bluer that day, but it''s not really something you can do on your own! LOL!<br/> <br/>CHALLENGE:  At first, I didn''t see a lot of rhythm, but the more I look at it, the more I see it and the more I like it.  Anyone who didn''t look closely may have scored this lower than it deserved. <br/> <br/>SUBJECT:  Again, interesting and very fun to "stare into".  It does have a cluttered feel, but then alot of things do, and are still just as interesting to look at.  The lamp post is a bit distracting to me, as is the fence.<br/> <br/>OVERALL:  A great photo that just needs a little cropping and maybe a color change on the sky showing through. Really though, this is a very FUN photo to stare into!  Great job.</td>', 'CRITIQUE CLUB LIGHTING:  Great use of the natural lighting - obviously, it would be great if the sky were bluer that day, but it''s not really something you can do on your own! LOL! CHALLENGE:  At first, I didn''t see a lot of rhythm, but the more I look at it, the more I see it and the more I like it.  Anyone who didn''t look closely may have scored this lower than it deserved.  SUBJECT:  Again, interesting and very fun to "stare into".  It does have a cluttered feel, but then alot of things do, and are still just as interesting to look at.  The lamp post is a bit distracting to me, as is the fence. OVERALL:  A great photo that just needs a little cropping and maybe a color change on the sky showing through. Really though, this is a very FUN photo to stare into!  Great job.', '2003-02-28 00:09:25.000000', NULL, 0),
(225824, 12359, 674, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club<br/> <br/>Composition The composition definitely meets the challenge as the tiles portray lovely rythmic lines.<br/>Exposure:<br/>Contrast &amp; Color are good.<br/> <br/>Suggestions for improvement: <br/>Take a crop from the middle to do away with the white line at the bottom, and the edge of the roof. Neither of these enhance the image!</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club Composition The composition definitely meets the challenge as the tiles portray lovely rythmic lines.Exposure:Contrast & Color are good. Suggestions for improvement: Take a crop from the middle to do away with the white line at the bottom, and the edge of the roof. Neither of these enhance the image!', '2003-02-28 01:08:58.000000', NULL, 0),
(225829, 12371, 811, '<td valign="top">CRITIQUE CLUB CRITIQUE<br/>by karmat<br/> <br/>COMPOSITION<br/>I agree about the centered composition.  It does show the pattern nicely.  I might suggest showing more of the green though.  It sets up a nice contrast with the yellow and blue, and the yellow diagonals lead the eyes out that side of the picture.  It just seems to feel off-balanced to me.  Maybe the white area with text needs to be further over to the left, leading more into the picture, or something of that nature.<br/> <br/>TECHNIQUE<br/>Great focus and lighting.  I cannot think of anything I would do to make this a better shot, technically.  (A lot of help I am, huh?)<br/> <br/>OVERALL EFFECT<br/>This is a great idea, but I am afraid some viewers might not catch the pattern quickly because it is not blatantly obvious that that is the "rhythm."  Technically, it is an awesome shot, but it does feel a bit unbalanced to me.</td>', 'CRITIQUE CLUB CRITIQUEby karmat COMPOSITIONI agree about the centered composition.  It does show the pattern nicely.  I might suggest showing more of the green though.  It sets up a nice contrast with the yellow and blue, and the yellow diagonals lead the eyes out that side of the picture.  It just seems to feel off-balanced to me.  Maybe the white area with text needs to be further over to the left, leading more into the picture, or something of that nature. TECHNIQUEGreat focus and lighting.  I cannot think of anything I would do to make this a better shot, technically.  (A lot of help I am, huh?) OVERALL EFFECTThis is a great idea, but I am afraid some viewers might not catch the pattern quickly because it is not blatantly obvious that that is the "rhythm."  Technically, it is an awesome shot, but it does feel a bit unbalanced to me.', '2003-02-28 01:15:35.000000', NULL, 0),
(225830, 12650, 674, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club<br/>Composition<br/>I love the way this is framed and feel that there is a definite rhythm to the way the snow is falling.  <br/>Sharpness: The soft focus adds to this image it doesn''t detract from it in my opinion<br/> <br/>Exposure:   <br/>Contrast &amp; Color: A little bit grey.<br/> <br/>Suggestions for improvement: <br/>Next time up the ev a stop and try iso 200.</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique ClubCompositionI love the way this is framed and feel that there is a definite rhythm to the way the snow is falling.  Sharpness: The soft focus adds to this image it doesn''t detract from it in my opinion Exposure:   Contrast & Color: A little bit grey. Suggestions for improvement: Next time up the ev a stop and try iso 200.', '2003-02-28 01:18:43.000000', NULL, 0),
(225847, 12759, 674, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club<br/>Firstly let me congratulate you on this image it was one of myfavorites of the rhythm challenge!<br/>The composition is excellent, the contrast and depth of field are great!<br/>Your lighting and focus are very hard to fault! The coppery colour of the strings fading off into the distance is great. All in all a Wow! All I can say is keep up the good work! A question do you play the guitar as well as you photograph them!</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique ClubFirstly let me congratulate you on this image it was one of myfavorites of the rhythm challenge!The composition is excellent, the contrast and depth of field are great!Your lighting and focus are very hard to fault! The coppery colour of the strings fading off into the distance is great. All in all a Wow! All I can say is keep up the good work! A question do you play the guitar as well as you photograph them!', '2003-02-28 05:16:59.000000', NULL, 0),
(226000, 12483, 811, '<td valign="top">CRITIQUE CLUB CRITIQUE<br/>by karmat<br/> <br/>COMPOSITION<br/>A neat idea for subject matter here.  The lines in the snow help to draw the eyes upwards into the frame.  That said, I almost think you could have cropped just the patterns on the left and it would have been a bit more noticeable.  As it is, the drifts compete for attention.<br/> <br/>TECHNIQUE<br/>You have done well at getting the snow to be a pure white.  The drifts though are just kinda gray.  If you could have taken it at another time of day, maybe the shadows would have been more distinct or darker.  Also, if there had been some way to show the scale, right now this could be a mountain ridge from the air (okay, maybe not really), or your front yard.<br/> <br/>OVERALL EFFECT<br/>This is a pleasing picture to look at and the drifts create a very graceful pattern that is relaxing to look at.  Good work, and good luck in the future.</td>', 'CRITIQUE CLUB CRITIQUEby karmat COMPOSITIONA neat idea for subject matter here.  The lines in the snow help to draw the eyes upwards into the frame.  That said, I almost think you could have cropped just the patterns on the left and it would have been a bit more noticeable.  As it is, the drifts compete for attention. TECHNIQUEYou have done well at getting the snow to be a pure white.  The drifts though are just kinda gray.  If you could have taken it at another time of day, maybe the shadows would have been more distinct or darker.  Also, if there had been some way to show the scale, right now this could be a mountain ridge from the air (okay, maybe not really), or your front yard. OVERALL EFFECTThis is a pleasing picture to look at and the drifts create a very graceful pattern that is relaxing to look at.  Good work, and good luck in the future.', '2003-02-28 14:07:54.000000', NULL, 0),
(226079, 12699, 4493, '<td valign="top">Critique Club Comment:<br/> <br/>First off, congrats on the win! This is an excellent shot with many great qualities. First I like the feel of the background. The dark stripes with the brighter ones really have a nice feel to them. Not only do they create the nice reflection in your glass, but they give the shot depth. The glass is unique in shape and has a nice curve to it, this rhythm coupled with the reflection of the background in it draws my eye in. It is more art then photography with the refractions and curves presented. Working with light on glass is not an easily done. I did a glass shot for yellow and had some issues with mine. You have one bright spot on the neck of the glass, but not large enough to be a distraction. The thing that does catch my eye is that you may have oversharpened. You have some stair stepping on the edges of the glass. Other then that, your exposure is excellent! <br/> <br/>I like it when a photographer creates more than just a photo, but a piece of art!</td>', 'Critique Club Comment: First off, congrats on the win! This is an excellent shot with many great qualities. First I like the feel of the background. The dark stripes with the brighter ones really have a nice feel to them. Not only do they create the nice reflection in your glass, but they give the shot depth. The glass is unique in shape and has a nice curve to it, this rhythm coupled with the reflection of the background in it draws my eye in. It is more art then photography with the refractions and curves presented. Working with light on glass is not an easily done. I did a glass shot for yellow and had some issues with mine. You have one bright spot on the neck of the glass, but not large enough to be a distraction. The thing that does catch my eye is that you may have oversharpened. You have some stair stepping on the edges of the glass. Other then that, your exposure is excellent!  I like it when a photographer creates more than just a photo, but a piece of art!', '2003-02-28 17:37:49.000000', NULL, 0),
(226080, 12596, 4493, '<td valign="top">Critique Club Comment:<br/> <br/>Very well done shot! What draws me in is not the spools, but the cloth they are on. This, I think, brings the whole shot together. It''s this element that makes me, the viewer, wonder who is using this thread, what are they sewing, who are they sewing it for? The human element was brought in without even using a person. The spools and support they are on coupled with the grey tones in the cloth make for a wonderful subtle prop for the great colors of thread. Your choice of thread colors was great. They aren''t too bright to overpower the photo, but are enough to jump out and grab the viewer''s attention. Your lighting is good as well as DOF, this creates a pleasant exposure. I think the background crumple of fabric might have been relayed differently in a more simple pattern that flows better with the curves of the spools.<br/> <br/>This is very well done and is pleasing to look at.</td>', 'Critique Club Comment: Very well done shot! What draws me in is not the spools, but the cloth they are on. This, I think, brings the whole shot together. It''s this element that makes me, the viewer, wonder who is using this thread, what are they sewing, who are they sewing it for? The human element was brought in without even using a person. The spools and support they are on coupled with the grey tones in the cloth make for a wonderful subtle prop for the great colors of thread. Your choice of thread colors was great. They aren''t too bright to overpower the photo, but are enough to jump out and grab the viewer''s attention. Your lighting is good as well as DOF, this creates a pleasant exposure. I think the background crumple of fabric might have been relayed differently in a more simple pattern that flows better with the curves of the spools. This is very well done and is pleasing to look at.', '2003-02-28 17:45:42.000000', NULL, 0),
(226181, 12496, 3540, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club<br/>By Inspzil, again!!<br/> <br/>Composition - I find this subject pretty appealing, but there are definitely some issues that I feel hurt the street value of this photo.  I think the lighting being inconsistent from the bottom of the pipes to the top is a big issue.  They look sort of blue on the bottom, true color with highlights in the middle, and sort of dull toward the top.  The shadowed part at the top I think is the biggest sticking point for me.  I''m not too crazy about the crop.  I''m looking for something sort of framing them in, distinguishing these pipes from just the regular part of the wall.  Its hard to tell if where they end on the pic is really the end, or just a spot where it was convenient to cut it.  The pipes on the right being cut off at the top is a bit of a problem as well.<br/> <br/>Technical stuff - Honestly, about as good as a person can take a picture.  exposure, dof, focus, all there with bells on.  Terrific.<br/> <br/>Overall - The crop of this pic really leaves me hanging.  This picture makes me think that its a much bigger picture, but here''s the inset for the details of the pipe.  It seems rather incomplete to me.  The pipes are great as is the wood, but it needs something to make it whole.  Interesting photo, but it lacks the visual impact it needs to be really successful - Good luck!!  Bob (again)</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique ClubBy Inspzil, again!! Composition - I find this subject pretty appealing, but there are definitely some issues that I feel hurt the street value of this photo.  I think the lighting being inconsistent from the bottom of the pipes to the top is a big issue.  They look sort of blue on the bottom, true color with highlights in the middle, and sort of dull toward the top.  The shadowed part at the top I think is the biggest sticking point for me.  I''m not too crazy about the crop.  I''m looking for something sort of framing them in, distinguishing these pipes from just the regular part of the wall.  Its hard to tell if where they end on the pic is really the end, or just a spot where it was convenient to cut it.  The pipes on the right being cut off at the top is a bit of a problem as well. Technical stuff - Honestly, about as good as a person can take a picture.  exposure, dof, focus, all there with bells on.  Terrific. Overall - The crop of this pic really leaves me hanging.  This picture makes me think that its a much bigger picture, but here''s the inset for the details of the pipe.  It seems rather incomplete to me.  The pipes are great as is the wood, but it needs something to make it whole.  Interesting photo, but it lacks the visual impact it needs to be really successful - Good luck!!  Bob (again)', '2003-02-28 21:40:56.000000', NULL, 0),
(226222, 12673, 3540, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club<br/>By Inspzil<br/> <br/>Composition - This is a really neat capture.  I like the colors on the left duck.  They seem a little underexposed but I don''t think that''s the case.  I think they are just slightly out of focus due to their movement.  They are really well framed.  Both being in roughly the same position is a great touch and the biggest selling point of this photo IMO.  The downside to this pic is the branches being out of focus.  I think you probably would''ve been better off if the branches were moving a lot, then it would convey their motion better.  You kinda got stuck in the in between, between a panning motion shot and a stop action motion shot where the background has to be a blur or crystal clear.  Personally I think its pretty good as is, but could definitely improve if the background fit into one of the "approved" techniques on DPC.  The panning shot is still under review (according to my shots anyway :) ).<br/> <br/>Technical - This image lacks a little of the crispness that it needs.  I don''t feel it needs to be the clearest image we''ve ever seen on DPC but for the most part, voters in these parts generally frown on any less than crystal clear.  The slight "underexposure" of the birds heads is probably not correctable within DPC rules.  Some of the function might be a little bit due to the DOF if the camera is close to the subjects.  I don''t believe this to be true in this case.  <br/> <br/>Overall - I think this picture is pretty underrated.  It''s not a bad picture and I think it deserves a little better score.  I think you took a lot of hits for the background not being totally focused which I don''t think is totally justified.  Unfortunately I can''t change your score but I hope you found at least one thing in here that will help you on a future picture.  Good Luck with your photos - Inspzil</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique ClubBy Inspzil Composition - This is a really neat capture.  I like the colors on the left duck.  They seem a little underexposed but I don''t think that''s the case.  I think they are just slightly out of focus due to their movement.  They are really well framed.  Both being in roughly the same position is a great touch and the biggest selling point of this photo IMO.  The downside to this pic is the branches being out of focus.  I think you probably would''ve been better off if the branches were moving a lot, then it would convey their motion better.  You kinda got stuck in the in between, between a panning motion shot and a stop action motion shot where the background has to be a blur or crystal clear.  Personally I think its pretty good as is, but could definitely improve if the background fit into one of the "approved" techniques on DPC.  The panning shot is still under review (according to my shots anyway :) ). Technical - This image lacks a little of the crispness that it needs.  I don''t feel it needs to be the clearest image we''ve ever seen on DPC but for the most part, voters in these parts generally frown on any less than crystal clear.  The slight "underexposure" of the birds heads is probably not correctable within DPC rules.  Some of the function might be a little bit due to the DOF if the camera is close to the subjects.  I don''t believe this to be true in this case.   Overall - I think this picture is pretty underrated.  It''s not a bad picture and I think it deserves a little better score.  I think you took a lot of hits for the background not being totally focused which I don''t think is totally justified.  Unfortunately I can''t change your score but I hope you found at least one thing in here that will help you on a future picture.  Good Luck with your photos - Inspzil', '2003-03-01 00:01:15.000000', NULL, 0),
(226357, 12646, 472, '<td valign="top">hey bod, late to the table i am, but this is truly an excellent image.  i had no idea the s602 was capable of such sharp detailed macros :) since you got that camera you have been rocking lately.  keep it up :).</td>', 'hey bod, late to the table i am, but this is truly an excellent image.  i had no idea the s602 was capable of such sharp detailed macros :) since you got that camera you have been rocking lately.  keep it up :).', '2003-03-01 12:09:14.000000', NULL, 0),
(226358, 12566, 472, '<td valign="top">great job! :)</td>', 'great job! :)', '2003-03-01 12:10:07.000000', NULL, 0),
(226406, 12603, 527, '<td valign="top">Critique Club critique by Tim Jensen<br/> <br/>As others have said this is a little soft on the focus. You may have had to crop some of the image to get it framed properly, plus, being in the boat probably did help too much.<br/> <br/>I still like the image quite a bit. There is nice rhythm and order to the scene and the composition has a way of keeping my eyes moving around effectively. I also like the way it is divided into sections horiontally helping to convey the sense of depth.<br/> <br/>This would probably be worth re-taking with a polarizer and either a steadier hand or a faster shutter speed. The polarizer will help deepen the blue in the sky and water and maybe provide a little more color to the rest of the scene.<br/> <br/>It definitely meets the challenge and considering that it is slightly soft I think you did a nice job with presenting the photo. You chose a good image to begin with. The mat is tastefully done as well. <br/> <br/>Good job Paul.<br/> <br/>T</td>', 'Critique Club critique by Tim Jensen As others have said this is a little soft on the focus. You may have had to crop some of the image to get it framed properly, plus, being in the boat probably did help too much. I still like the image quite a bit. There is nice rhythm and order to the scene and the composition has a way of keeping my eyes moving around effectively. I also like the way it is divided into sections horiontally helping to convey the sense of depth. This would probably be worth re-taking with a polarizer and either a steadier hand or a faster shutter speed. The polarizer will help deepen the blue in the sky and water and maybe provide a little more color to the rest of the scene. It definitely meets the challenge and considering that it is slightly soft I think you did a nice job with presenting the photo. You chose a good image to begin with. The mat is tastefully done as well.  Good job Paul. T', '2003-03-01 15:31:20.000000', NULL, 0),
(226439, 12654, 1280, '<td valign="top">Critique Club Comments by Grayce<br/> <br/>Sher, This is very eye-catching.  The perspective works to lead my eyes throughout the image. I like how it curves up near the top.  Very well composed.<br/>The interplay of shadows and light keep it interesting and rythmic.<br/>Adding the gaussian was a very good idea, that contributes to the overall textural effect.<br/>I do wonder if leaving the rusty color in it would have been even better, but black and white works too.<br/>Good job, and I look forward to more of your images.<br/>Regards,<br/> <br/>Grayce</td>', 'Critique Club Comments by Grayce Sher, This is very eye-catching.  The perspective works to lead my eyes throughout the image. I like how it curves up near the top.  Very well composed.The interplay of shadows and light keep it interesting and rythmic.Adding the gaussian was a very good idea, that contributes to the overall textural effect.I do wonder if leaving the rusty color in it would have been even better, but black and white works too.Good job, and I look forward to more of your images.Regards, Grayce', '2003-03-01 17:29:33.000000', NULL, 0),
(226513, 12373, 3280, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club };-) </b> <br/> <br/><b>Initial thoughts </b> <br/>Just my luck to critique your shot two weeks in a row! I wonder what it is, meets the challenge, seems a bit over sharpened.<br/> <br/><b>Composition/ Content </b> <br/>I looked at this quite a few times and never really figured out what I was looking at until I read your notes. I think that this certainly has "Rhythm" but somehow missed the boat a bit.  I like the contrast and the lighting but I think the refelecitons in the heads of the nails take away from the overall shot even as an abstract.  <br/> <br/><b>Camera Work - Technical</b> <br/>The thing I like best about the shot is the way you sank some of the nails to make a valley of shadow. It works well the way it runs from the top left corner but it does take away some of the focus in the shadows.<br/> <br/><b>Digital Processing - Technical </b> <br/>I can''t tell whether this is a bit over sharpened or whether it is just the glare from the lights in the nail heads. Either way it has a rather grainy feel to it.  I''m not sure if that was the intention but I think it is a little distracting. <br/> <br/><b>Fits The Challenge </b> <br/>Fits the challenge well. <br/> <br/><b>My Opinion On The Photo </b> <br/>I think you took a shot of a pretty tough subject. It has a rhythmic feel to it but there are too many other things going on in the shot to really impart that. Good shot but perhaps not one of your best that we''ve come to expect!  <br/> <br/>I would be happy to talk further about this shot if you would like to contact me.<br/><b>DougPaz</b> <br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club };-)   Initial thoughts  Just my luck to critique your shot two weeks in a row! I wonder what it is, meets the challenge, seems a bit over sharpened. Composition/ Content  I looked at this quite a few times and never really figured out what I was looking at until I read your notes. I think that this certainly has "Rhythm" but somehow missed the boat a bit.  I like the contrast and the lighting but I think the refelecitons in the heads of the nails take away from the overall shot even as an abstract.   Camera Work - Technical The thing I like best about the shot is the way you sank some of the nails to make a valley of shadow. It works well the way it runs from the top left corner but it does take away some of the focus in the shadows. Digital Processing - Technical  I can''t tell whether this is a bit over sharpened or whether it is just the glare from the lights in the nail heads. Either way it has a rather grainy feel to it.  I''m not sure if that was the intention but I think it is a little distracting.  Fits The Challenge  Fits the challenge well.  My Opinion On The Photo  I think you took a shot of a pretty tough subject. It has a rhythmic feel to it but there are too many other things going on in the shot to really impart that. Good shot but perhaps not one of your best that we''ve come to expect!   I would be happy to talk further about this shot if you would like to contact me.DougPaz ', '2003-03-01 23:15:52.000000', NULL, 0),
(226519, 12603, 1031, '<td valign="top">Thanks for your comments all. I was actually on the opposite dock, so I don''t have the excuse of the boat''s motion --  I blurred it all by myself. I was on autofocus and I''m pretty sure it picked the far background instead of the birds.<br/>Also I had to crop pretty tight, so I may have lost some resolution/clarity there. There were two more birds on the rail to the left, but they had turned a little, so I abandoned the horizontal framing, which would have been a stronger composition, in favor of the vertical with fewer (but better aligned) birds. I tried a little more sharpening but it resulted in an unacceptable amount of fringing.</td>', 'Thanks for your comments all. I was actually on the opposite dock, so I don''t have the excuse of the boat''s motion --  I blurred it all by myself. I was on autofocus and I''m pretty sure it picked the far background instead of the birds.Also I had to crop pretty tight, so I may have lost some resolution/clarity there. There were two more birds on the rail to the left, but they had turned a little, so I abandoned the horizontal framing, which would have been a stronger composition, in favor of the vertical with fewer (but better aligned) birds. I tried a little more sharpening but it resulted in an unacceptable amount of fringing.', '2003-03-02 00:14:41.000000', NULL, 0),
(226615, 12578, 3540, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club<br/>By Inspzil<br/> <br/>Composition - You could''ve used this photo for any number of recent challenges, yellow and perspective come to mind right away.  The sky looks a gorgeous blue color... too much so even.  The sky is pixelating probably from oversaturation.  The subject has very good colors and is a pretty good candidate for a rhythm shot.  The building this is attached to provides a good distraction for this pic.  Unfortunately we aren''t looking for "good" distractions and actually is a bit of an eyesore.  I am sure it HAS to be there to hold the tower up.  The tower would''ve been great if it could''ve been standing there on its own.  It does have some great colors and is well worth taking pics of.  <br/> <br/>Technical - Nice clear shot!  Very well taken.  Good exposure and DOF.  The big hangup in the processing area is the pixelating sky.  The rest of the colors seem like they are ok.  The angles of this shot are quite puzzling with neither of the structures being vertical nor horizontal on their axes.  I''m not saying that''s bad, but perhaps a bit "curious" instead.<br/> <br/>Overall - A pretty nice shot with very nice color (except for the sky).  Very well taken picture with some abstract angles.  I think it meets the challenge pretty well.  Nice pic, good luck in upcoming challenges - Bob</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique ClubBy Inspzil Composition - You could''ve used this photo for any number of recent challenges, yellow and perspective come to mind right away.  The sky looks a gorgeous blue color... too much so even.  The sky is pixelating probably from oversaturation.  The subject has very good colors and is a pretty good candidate for a rhythm shot.  The building this is attached to provides a good distraction for this pic.  Unfortunately we aren''t looking for "good" distractions and actually is a bit of an eyesore.  I am sure it HAS to be there to hold the tower up.  The tower would''ve been great if it could''ve been standing there on its own.  It does have some great colors and is well worth taking pics of.   Technical - Nice clear shot!  Very well taken.  Good exposure and DOF.  The big hangup in the processing area is the pixelating sky.  The rest of the colors seem like they are ok.  The angles of this shot are quite puzzling with neither of the structures being vertical nor horizontal on their axes.  I''m not saying that''s bad, but perhaps a bit "curious" instead. Overall - A pretty nice shot with very nice color (except for the sky).  Very well taken picture with some abstract angles.  I think it meets the challenge pretty well.  Nice pic, good luck in upcoming challenges - Bob', '2003-03-02 10:20:32.000000', NULL, 0),
(226778, 12566, 1143, '<td valign="top">The composition is nice. I like the diagonals. Lighting is good. Any more exposure, and you''d lose some detail in the highlight areas. As is, there''s a good range betwen light and dark. I really like the decision to make this blue. It''s a little more understated than yellow and puts the focus more on the repetition and lines while still adding interest.<br/>Biggest gripe would be the soft focus in the upper left corner. If you had used a different angle so that it was obvious that some threads are closer to the camera then a shallow dof could be useful. As is, it looks like they are on the same plane so it doesn''t work to have a section out of focus. The little hairs don''t really bother me. You have such great focus in the center, and it IS a macro. The whole point is to show things as they aren''t usually seen - beauty in the details - and I think you achieved that.<br/>For this challenge, it''s a great shot. However, for a portfolio... I think that you should do some sort of series. I can imagine this hanging in a grouping of 3 or 4 on a well. Each shot being a different color and showing a similar sort of close-up that focuses on texture and lines. Maybe you can keep that in mind and make it a work in progress.<br/>Congratulations on the high score. It''s a very simple, clean, and effective photo.<br/></td>', 'The composition is nice. I like the diagonals. Lighting is good. Any more exposure, and you''d lose some detail in the highlight areas. As is, there''s a good range betwen light and dark. I really like the decision to make this blue. It''s a little more understated than yellow and puts the focus more on the repetition and lines while still adding interest.Biggest gripe would be the soft focus in the upper left corner. If you had used a different angle so that it was obvious that some threads are closer to the camera then a shallow dof could be useful. As is, it looks like they are on the same plane so it doesn''t work to have a section out of focus. The little hairs don''t really bother me. You have such great focus in the center, and it IS a macro. The whole point is to show things as they aren''t usually seen - beauty in the details - and I think you achieved that.For this challenge, it''s a great shot. However, for a portfolio... I think that you should do some sort of series. I can imagine this hanging in a grouping of 3 or 4 on a well. Each shot being a different color and showing a similar sort of close-up that focuses on texture and lines. Maybe you can keep that in mind and make it a work in progress.Congratulations on the high score. It''s a very simple, clean, and effective photo.', '2003-03-02 16:31:16.000000', NULL, 0),
(226840, 12329, 4493, '<td valign="top">Critique Club Comment:<br/> <br/>You have found a very interesting piece of architecture. The repeating columns and decorative stars do have a rhythm to them. With a shot like this one, the viewer is looking for balance. You have the elements available to make a balanced shot, but your crop has left more column on the left and cut off the one on the right. This leaves me feeling that this has a more of a snapshot feel to it. As you get more in tune with your shots, you''ll begin paying attention to your location in relation to the subject you''re shooting. As you move around you may realize that you can get a better perspective by moving left or right, or by kneeling or standing on something. This shot just needed you to move more to the right so you are standing directly in front of the middle column. This would have square the horizon and balanced the space on the left and right. Your white balance is good. The tones in the building look correct. You don''t have any areas that are overly bright or dark and the curtains appear sharp and in good light. You may have oversharpened your final version here. This can be seen in by the stairstepping in the window stars. <br/> <br/>This is a fine shot, it only needs a small amount of adjustments to make this really stand out for the viewer.</td>', 'Critique Club Comment: You have found a very interesting piece of architecture. The repeating columns and decorative stars do have a rhythm to them. With a shot like this one, the viewer is looking for balance. You have the elements available to make a balanced shot, but your crop has left more column on the left and cut off the one on the right. This leaves me feeling that this has a more of a snapshot feel to it. As you get more in tune with your shots, you''ll begin paying attention to your location in relation to the subject you''re shooting. As you move around you may realize that you can get a better perspective by moving left or right, or by kneeling or standing on something. This shot just needed you to move more to the right so you are standing directly in front of the middle column. This would have square the horizon and balanced the space on the left and right. Your white balance is good. The tones in the building look correct. You don''t have any areas that are overly bright or dark and the curtains appear sharp and in good light. You may have oversharpened your final version here. This can be seen in by the stairstepping in the window stars.  This is a fine shot, it only needs a small amount of adjustments to make this really stand out for the viewer.', '2003-03-02 19:49:34.000000', NULL, 0),
